2 CONT vte
2 CONT Maria Paola Buonaparte, the sixth child of Letizia Ramolino and Carlo Maria Buonaparte, Corsica's representative to the court of King Louis XVI, was born on 20 October 1780 in Ajaccio, Corsica.[3] She was popularly known as "Paoli
2 CONC na" in Italian. Her family soon took also a French spelling of their surname, Bonaparte. She is said to be Napoleon's favorite sister.[4] Little is known about her childhood, except that she received no formal education.[5] Follow
2 CONC ing Carlo's death in 1785, the family was plunged into poverty.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pauline's brother Lucien Bonaparte made seditious comments at the local Jacobin chapter in the summer of 1793, forcing the family to flee to the mainland. It was there on the mainland that she became known as "Paulette". The incom
2 CONC e the Bonapartes earned from their vineyards and other holdings on Corsica was interrupted by the British capture of the island in 1794.[6] Their economic situation became so dire that the Bonaparte women reportedly resorted to wa
2 CONC shing clothes for payment.[7] Regardless, they received, like other French refugees from Corsica, a stipend from the French government. From their landing place, Toulon, they moved to Marseille, where General Napoleon Bonaparte, h
2 CONC er elder brother, introduced her to Louis-Marie Stanislas Fréron, the proconsul of Marseille.[8] He intended them to marry, but Letizia objected.[6] Napoleon, despite the fact that Pauline loved Stanislas, married her to General C
2 CONC harles Leclerc in French-occupied Milan on 14 June 1797.[9] Napoleon returned to Paris and delegated the office of commander-in-chief of the French army in Italy to his brother-in-law.[10] Pauline gave birth to a boy, Dermide Loui
2 CONC s Napoleon, on 20 April 1798.[11] In celebration, General Leclerc acquired a property outside Novellara worth 160,000 French francs.[12] Ill-health forced Leclerc to resign from his military post in October of the same year; he wa
2 CONC s transferred to Paris. Leclerc was again relocated upon arrival, this time to Brittany. Pauline stayed in Paris with Dermide.[13] Laure de Permond—the future Duchesse d'Abrantès—and her mother welcomed Pauline into their salon at
2 CONC  the rue Saint-Croix.[14] Napoleon seized power in Coup of Brumaire in November 1799: deposing the Directory, he pronounced himself First Consul.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint-Domingue
2 CONT Saint-Domingue in the West Indies (modern-day Haiti) had been a French colony since 1697, but had been in rebellion against France since 1791. Napoleon wished to restore French authority there, and so organized an expedition. He p
2 CONC ut General Leclerc at its head, appointing him Governor-General of the island.[16] Leclerc, Dermide, and Pauline embarked for the colony from Brest on 14 December 1801.[17] Leclerc's fleet totaled 74 ships.[17] The gubernatorial f
2 CONC amily occupied the flagship, l'Océan.[17] After a 45-day journey, the fleet arrived in Le Cap harbour.[18] The Governor-General ordered General Christophe, who commanded a force of 5,000 soldiers, to resign Le Cap to French author
2 CONC ity.[19] After all attempts at conciliation failed, Leclerc attacked the town under cover of darkness. Christophe responded by razing Le Cap to the ground.[20] Pauline, meanwhile, was left aboard the flagship with their son. Accor
2 CONC ding to Leclerc, in a letter dated 5 March to Napoleon, "The disastrous events in the midst of which she [Pauline] found herself wore her down to the point of making her ill."[21] Leclerc succeeded in requisitioning the capitulati
2 CONC on of the rebel leader, Toussaint L'ouverture, in May.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, celebrations were dampened by the advent of yellow fever season: 25 generals and 25,000 soldiers died from the fever.[22] Leclerc had initially guaranteed that slavery, abolished by the Jacobin republic in 1794, would sta
2 CONC y proscribed; however, the inhabitants caught wind of its re-establishment in another French colony, neighbouring Guadeloupe, in July.[23] The French government had eliminated slavery in May. As a result, the indigenous residents 
2 CONC of Saint-Domingue planned an insurrection for September 16.[24] Black troops in Leclerc's army defected to their old commanders, and the Governor-General had a mere 2,000 men against the rebels' 10,000.[25] Leclerc, fearing for Pa
2 CONC uline's safety, gave express orders to Jacques de Norvin, a sergeant, to remove Pauline from Saint-Domingue at a moment's notice,[26] but these precautions proved unnecessary when Leclerc defeated the insurgents.
2 CONT
2 CONT The climate was taking its toll on Pauline's health. She could no longer walk and was compelled to a "reclining position" for several hours a day.[27] Both she and Dermide suffered from spells of yellow fever.[28] She did, however
2 CONC , find time to take numerous lovers, including several of her husband's soldiers, and developed a reputation for "Bacchanalian promiscuity."[29]
2 CONT
2 CONT Leclerc attempted to convince Pauline to return to Paris in August.[30] She consented on the condition that "he [Leclerc]...give me 100,000 francs." When the Governor-General refused, she elected to stay in Saint-Domingue; observi
2 CONC ng that unlike in Paris, "Here, I reign like Josephine [Napoleon's wife]; I hold first place."[31]
2 CONT
2 CONT To occupy herself, Pauline compiled a collection of local flora and established a menagerie, inhabited by native animals.[31]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 October 1802, Leclerc fell ill. A doctor from the military hospital in Le Cap diagnosed him with a fever "caused by the bodily and mental hardships that the general [Leclerc] had suffered." Biographer Flora Fraser believes t
2 CONC hat his symptoms were consistent with those of yellow fever.[32] He died on 1 November. Seven days later, Pauline, Dermide, and Leclerc's remains were hastily ferried back to mainland France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Borghese
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrait of Pauline by François-Joseph Kinson, 1808
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrait of Pauline by Marie-Guillemine Benoist, 1808
2 CONT
2 CONT Venus Victrix, by Antonio Canova, depicting Princess Borghese as a deity, created 1804-1808. It was commissioned by her husband.[33]
2 CONT Pauline reached the Bay of Toulon on 1 January 1803. That same day she wrote to Napoleon: "I have brought with me the remains of my poor Leclerc. Pity poor Pauline, who is truly unhappy."[34]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 11 February, she arrived in the capital, where Napoleon made arrangements for her to lodge with their brother Joseph.[35] Parisian rumour had it that she extracted gold and jewels from the indigenous peoples in Saint-Domingue a
2 CONC nd brought the treasure back in Leclerc's sarcophagus, but this was not the case.[36] She inherited 700,000 francs in liquid capital and assets from Leclerc.[37]
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiring of life with Joseph, Pauline went about acquiring Hôtel Charost from the duchess to whom it belonged. She confided in a friend that she "was bored" with the code of mourning outlined in the First Consul's civil code, compel
2 CONC ling her to withdraw from Parisian society, which, before her time in Saint-Domingue, had had her at its center.[38] Napoleon did not wish her to remain unmarried for long; he tried—but failed—to betroth her to the Duke of Lodi an
2 CONC d Vice-President of the Napoleonic Republic of Italy, Francesco Melzi d'Eril.[38] Pope Pius VII's envoy, Giovanni Battista Caprara, suggested Camillo Borghese, 6th Prince of Sulmona, a Roman noble.[39] The First Consul believed th
2 CONC e union would consolidate ties with French-occupied Italy, where animosity toward the aggressor was rife.[40] That, combined with pressure from her brothers Joseph and Lucien, induced her to marry him. The marriage contract brough
2 CONC t Camillo a dowry of 500,000 francs; to Pauline, it brought 300,000 francs worth of jewelry and the use of the Borghese family diamonds.[41] On 28 August 1803, they were married by Caprara, but without the knowledge of Napoleon, w
2 CONC ho had wanted a November wedding for mourning protocol's sake. Upon discovering Pauline's deceit, he refused to acknowledge her new title: "Please understand, Madame, that there is no princess where I am." A civil ceremony was hel
2 CONC d in November to confirm the marriage. However, Pauline continued her extramarital affairs, including an affair with the violinist Niccolò Paganini.[29]
2 CONT
2 CONT Camillo, Pauline, and Dermide arrived in Rome on 14 November. Pauline, anxious to learn how to behave in Roman society, received tutorship in deportment and dancing.[42] Biographer William Carlton suggests that Pauline— a minor no
2 CONC ble from Corsica—would never have made such an advantageous match if it were it not for Napoleon's political eminence.[43] Pauline's initial amity toward Camillo soon morphed into dislike.[44] Her son Dermide, always a delicate ch
2 CONC ild, died on 14 August 1804 in the Aldobrandini villa in Frascati, after a violent fever and convulsions. Three years later, in 1807, his remains were moved next to those of his father in the park grounds of the Château de Montgob
2 CONC ert.[45]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Napoleon's fall
2 CONT In 1806, Napoleon made his sister sovereign Princess and Duchess of Guastalla; however, she soon sold the duchy to Parma for six million francs, keeping only the title of Princess of Guastalla. Pauline fell into temporary disfavor
2 CONC  with her brother because of her hostility to his second wife, Empress Marie Louise, but when Napoleon's fortune failed, Pauline showed herself more loyal than any of his other sisters and brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Napoleon's fall, Pauline liquidated all of her assets and moved to Elba, using that money to better Napoleon's condition. She was the only Bonaparte sibling to visit her brother during his exile on Elba. Her home in Paris, th
2 CONC e Hôtel de Charost, was sold to the British government and used by the Duke of Wellington as his official residence during his tenure as British Ambassador to France. Today the house is still the home of the British ambassador.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Waterloo Pauline moved to Rome, where she enjoyed the protection of Pope Pius VII (who once was her brother's prisoner), as did her mother, Letizia, (then at a palace on the Piazza Venezia) and other members of the Bonaparte
2 CONC  family. Pauline lived in a villa between the Porta Pia and the Porta Salaria that was called Villa Paolina after her and decorated in the Egyptomania style she favored. Her husband, Camillo, lived in the Palazzo Borghese, but the
2 CONC n moved to Florence to distance himself from her and had a ten-year relationship with a mistress. Even so, Pauline persuaded the Pope to convince the prince to take her back only three months before her death from pulmonary tuberc
2 CONC ulosis. She died in his Palazzo Salviati-Borghese in Florence.[46][29]
2 CONT
2 CONT Health
2 CONT Pauline was of frail health for much of her life, probably due to salpingitis.[47] She died on 9 June 1825 at the age of forty-four at the Palazzo Salviati-Borghese in Florence, the cause of death being given as 'tumor on the stom
2 CONC ach'[48] but it may have been pulmonary tuberculosis.[49]
2 CONT
2 CONT In popular culture
2 CONT Simone Genevois plays her in the 1927 silent film Napoléon.
2 CONT Gladys Holland plays her in the 1952 Haitian-located action film Lydia Bailey.[50]
2 CONT Charlotte Austin plays her in the 1954 film Désirée.
2 CONT Gianna Maria Canale plays her in the 1955 French film Napoléon.
2 CONT Claudia Cardinale plays her in the 1960 French film Austerlitz.
2 CONT Laura Valenzuela plays her in the 1961 Italian-French film Madame.
2 CONT Gina Lollobrigida plays her in the 1962 French film Venere imperiale.[50]
2 CONT Maria Rosaria Omaggio plays her in the 1979 French miniseries Joséphine ou la Comédie des ambitions.
2 CONT Ione Skye plays her in the 1987 miniseries Napoleon and Josephine: A Love Story.
2 CONT Constance Dollé plays her in the 2002 miniseries Napoléon.
2 CONT Yelena Podkaminskaya plays her in the 2006 Russian telenovela Adjutants of Love.
2 CONT She is the main character in the 2019 video game Banner of the Maid
2 CONT
2 CONT Paulina Bonaparte was the first sovereign Duchess of Guastalla; a Princess of France; and the Princess consort of Sulmona and Rossano. She was the sixth child of Letizia Ramolino and Carlo Buonaparte, Corsica's representative to t
2 CONC he court of King Louis XVI of France. Her elder brother, Napoleon, was the first Emperor of the French. Firstly, she married Charles Leclerc, a French general -- a union ended by his death in 1802 -- and secondly Camillo Borghese,
2 CONC  6th Prince of Sulmona. By the former she had her only child, Demide, who died before reaching adulthood. She was the only Bonaparte sibling to visit Napoleon on his principality-cum-prison, Elba.
2 CONT
