1 NOTE {geni:about_me} His full name was Alexandre François Marie de Beauharnais. He served in the American Revolution. He was Deputy of the Nobility of Blois to the States-General. During the French Revolution, he was President of the A
2 CONC ssembly and Major-General of the Army of the Rhine. He was arrested in March 1794, and executed during the Reign of Terror.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1786, the Genealogist of His Majesty's Orders of Chivalry wrote of the Beauharnais family, "Monsieur [Alexandre] de Beauharnais is not entitled to the Court Honors which he solicits. His is a good middle-class family of Orléans
2 CONC  which an old genealogy filed in the office of the Order of the Holy Ghost describes as having been originally known under the name of Beauvi, which it later abandoned to take that of Beauharnais. Some of its members were merchant
2 CONC s, magistrates and minor judges in the tribunals of the same town, and other were counselors to the Parliament of Paris. One of its branches, known as Lords of La Bretesche, was condemned by a judgment of M. de Machault, Intendant
2 CONC  of Orléans, on 4 April, 1667, as usurping a title of nobility, to pay 2,000 francs fine, which was reduced to 1,000."
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms of Beauharnais: Argent a fesse Sable, in chief three martlets of the last. Reference: Alexandre de Beauharnais, (1760-1794), Martinique and the Orléanais, France: (husband of the future Empress Josephine).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Wikipedia ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre François Marie de Beauharnais http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandre_de_Beauharnais 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre, vicomte de Beauharnais http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandre,_vicomte_de_Beauharnais
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre François Marie de Beauharnais, Vicomte de Beauharnais (28 May 1760 – 23 July 1794) was a French political figure and general during the French Revolution. He was the first husband of Joséphine Tascher de la Pagerie, who 
2 CONC later married Napoleon Bonaparte and became Empress of the First Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre was arrested in March 1794, and following his sentence of death during the Reign of Terror, was executed by guillotine in Paris's Place de la Révolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT His paternal grandparents Claude de Beauharnais (1680 – 1738) and Renée Hardouineau (1696 – 1744) were married in La Rochelle during 1713. His father François de Beauharnais, Marquess de la La Ferté-Beauharnais (1714 – 1800) serve
2 CONC d as Governor of Martinique. Alexandre was the third of three sons born to him by his first wife Marie Henriette Pyvart de Chastullé (1722 – 1767) - the first died in infancy, and the second was Francis VI of Beauharnais. His fath
2 CONC er was remarried in 1796 to Eugenie de Tascher de la Pagerie (1739 – 1803).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre was born in Fort-Royal (today's Fort-de-France), Martinique. On 13 December 1779 in Paris, France, he married Joséphine Tascher de la Pagerie, the future Empress of France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Eugène de Beauharnais (3 September 1781 – 21 February 1824).
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Hortense de Beauharnais (10 April 1783 – 5 October 1837), later mother of Napoleon III of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre fought in Louis XVI's army in the American Revolutionary War. He was later deputy of the noblesse in the Estates-General, and was president of the National Constituent Assembly from 19 June to 3 July 1791 and from 31 Jul
2 CONC y to 14 August 1791. Made a general in 1792 (during the French Revolutionary Wars), he refused, in June 1793, to become Minister of War. He was named General-in-Chief of the Army of the Rhine in 1793.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 March 1794, the Committee of General Security ordered his arrest. Accused of having poorly defended Mainz during the Siege of Mainz in 1793, and considered an aristocratic "suspect", he was jailed in the Carmes prison and sen
2 CONC tenced to death during the Reign of Terror. His wife, Josephine de Beauharnais, was jailed in the same prison on 21 April 1794, but she was freed after three months, thanks to the trial of Maximilien Robespierre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre was guillotined, together with his cousin Augustin, on the Place de la Révolution (today's Place de la Concorde) in Paris, only five days before the deposition and execution of Robespierre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his son, he became an ancestor of today's Royal Houses of Belgium, Denmark, Norway and Sweden.
2 CONT
