1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Archduke of Austria 1768 - 1792
2 CONT *Holy Roman Emperor 1792 - 1806
2 CONT *Emperor of Austria 1792 - 1835
2 CONT *Was the last Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT ===Golden Fleece - Knights: Austrian Branch===
2 CONT '''Links:'''
2 CONT *[http://thepeerage.com/p11055.htm#i110546 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/D/per_page.php?id=5343 Geneall]
2 CONT *'''Wikipedia:''' [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor English] [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_II._(HRR)_  Deutsch]
2 CONT *'''King of Hungary and Croatia, King of Bohemia:''' Reign 1 March 1792 – 2 March 1835 Coronation 6 June 1792, Buda / 9 August 1792, Prague 
2 CONT >'''Predecessor:''' [http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000000350830758 Leopold II] '''Successor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/index/6000000000951436798 Ferdinand I] 
2 CONT *'''Holy Roman Emperor, King in Germany:''' Reign 5 July 1792 – 6 August 1806 Coronation 14 July 1792, Frankfurt  
2 CONT >'''Predecessor:''' [http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000000350830758 Leopold II] '''Successor:''' Office abolished 
2 CONT *'''Emperor of Austria:''' Reign 11 August 1804 – 2 March 1835 
2 CONT >'''Predecessor''' New creation '''Successor''' [http://www.geni.com/people/index/6000000000951436798 Ferdinand I] 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Francis II (German: Franz II, Erwählter Römischer Kaiser) (12 February 1768 – 2 March 1835) was the last Holy Roman Emperor, ruling from 1792 until 6 August 1806, when he dissolved the Holy Roman Empire after the disastrous defeat
2 CONC  of the Third Coalition by Napoleon at the Battle of Austerlitz. In 1804, he had founded the Austrian Empire and became Francis I (Franz I.), the first Emperor of Austria (Kaiser von Österreich), ruling from 1804 to 1835, so later
2 CONC  he was named the one and only Doppelkaiser (double emperor) in history. [1] For the two years between 1804 and 1806, Francis used the title and style by the grace of God elected Roman Emperor, always August, hereditary Emperor of
2 CONC  Austria and he was called the Emperor of both Germany and Austria. He was also Apostolic King of Hungary and Bohemia as Francis I. He also served as the first president of the German Confederation following its establishment in 1
2 CONC 815.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis I continued his leading role as an opponent of Napoleonic France in the Napoleonic Wars, and suffered several more defeats after Austerlitz. The proxy marriage of state of his daughter Marie Louise of Austria to Napoleon o
2 CONC n 10 March 1810 was assuredly his most severe defeat. After the abdication of Napoleon following the War of the Sixth Coalition, Austria participated as a leading member of the Holy Alliance at the Congress of Vienna, which was la
2 CONC rgely dominated by Francis's chancellor Klemens Wenzel, Prince von Metternich culminating in a new European map and the restoration of Francis' ancient dominions (except the Holy Roman Empire which was dissolved). Due to the estab
2 CONC lishment of the Concert of Europe, which largely resisted popular nationalist and liberal tendencies, Francis became viewed as a reactionary later in his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis was a son of Emperor Leopold II (1747–1792) and his wife Maria Luisa of Spain (1745–1792), daughter of Charles III of Spain. Francis was born in Florence, the capital of Tuscany, where his father reigned as Grand Duke from
2 CONC  1765–90. Though he had a happy childhood surrounded by his many siblings,[2] his family knew Francis was likely to be a future Emperor (his uncle Joseph had no surviving issue from either of his two marriages), and so in 1784 the
2 CONC  young Archduke was sent to the Imperial Court in Vienna to educate and prepare him for his future role.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Joseph II himself took charge of Francis's development. His disciplinarian regime was a stark contrast to the indulgent Florentine Court of Leopold. The Emperor wrote that Francis was "stunted in growth", "backward in bodi
2 CONC ly dexterity and deportment", and "neither more nor less than a spoiled mother's child". Joseph concluded that "…the manner in which he was treated for upwards of sixteen years could not but have confirmed him in the delusion that
2 CONC  the preservation of his own person was the only thing of importance."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph's martinet method of improving the young Francis were "fear and unpleasantness".[4] The young Archduke was isolated, the reasoning being that this would make him more self-sufficient as it was felt by Joseph that Francis "f
2 CONC ail[ed] to lead himself, to do his own thinking". Nonetheless, Francis greatly admired his uncle, if rather feared him. To complete his training, Francis was sent to join an army regiment in Hungary and he settled easily into the 
2 CONC routine of military life.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Joseph II in 1790, Francis's father became Emperor. He had an early taste of power while acting as Leopold's deputy in Vienna while the incoming Emperor traversed the Empire attempting to win back those alienate
2 CONC d by his brother's policies.[6] The strain told on Leopold and by the winter of 1791, he became ill. He gradually worsened throughout early 1792; on the afternoon of 1 March Leopold died, at the relatively young age of 44. Francis
2 CONC , just past his 24th birthday, was now Emperor, much sooner than he had expected.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor
2 CONT * https://hr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franjo_II.,_car_Svetog_Rimskog_Carstva
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis II (German: Franz II., Erwählter Römischer Kaiser) (12 February 1768 – 2 March 1835) was the last Holy Roman Emperor, ruling from 1792 until 6 August 1806, when he dissolved the Holy Roman Empire after the decisive defeat 
2 CONC at the hands of the First French Empire led by Napoleon at the Battle of Austerlitz. In 1804, he had founded the Austrian Empire and became Francis I (Franz I.), the first Emperor of Austria (Kaiser von Österreich), ruling from 18
2 CONC 04 to 1835, so later he was named the one and only Doppelkaiser (double emperor) in history.[1] For the two years between 1804 and 1806, Francis used the title and style by the grace of God elected Roman Emperor, ever Augustus, he
2 CONC reditary Emperor of Austria and he was called the Emperor of both the Holy Roman Empire and Austria. He was also Apostolic King of Hungary and Bohemia as Francis I. He also served as the first president of the German Confederation
2 CONC  following its establishment in 1815.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis II continued his leading role as an opponent of Napoleonic France in the Napoleonic Wars, and suffered several more defeats after Austerlitz. The proxy marriage of state of his daughter Marie Louise of Austria to Napoleon 
2 CONC on 10 March 1810 was arguably his most severe personal defeat. After the abdication of Napoleon following the War of the Sixth Coalition, Austria participated as a leading member of the Holy Alliance at the Congress of Vienna, whi
2 CONC ch was largely dominated by Francis's chancellor Klemens Wenzel, Prince von Metternich culminating in a new European map and the restoration of Francis' ancient dominions (except the Holy Roman Empire which was dissolved). Due to 
2 CONC the establishment of the Concert of Europe, which largely resisted popular nationalist and liberal tendencies, Francis became viewed as a reactionary later in his reign.
