1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Dudley_Stuart<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [ The Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lord Dudley Coutts Stuart''' 
2 CONT * Born on 11 January 1803 at London, England
2 CONT * Son of '''John Stuart, 1st Marquess of the County of Bute''' and '''Frances Coutts'''
2 CONT * Married '''Christine Charlotte Alexandrine Egypta Bonaparte''', daughter of Lucien Bonaparte, 1st Prince de Canino and Catherine Christine Elenore Boyer, on 20 July 1824.
2 CONT * Died on 17 November 1854 at age 51 at Stockholm, Sweden.
2 CONT * Graduated from Cambridge University, Cambridge, Cambridgeshire, England, in 1823 with a Master of Arts (M.A.).* * Held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Arundel between 1830 and 1837 and of Member of Parliament (M.P.
2 CONC ) for Marylebone between 1847 and 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Child of Lord Dudley Coutts Stuart and Christine Charlotte Alexandrine Egypta Bonaparte'''
2 CONT * Paul Amadeus Francis Coutts Stuart d. 1 Aug 1889
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Lord Dudley Coutts Stuart (11 January 1803, London – 17 November 1854, Stockholm) was a British politician. He was the youngest son of John Stuart, 1st Marquess of Bute, and his second wife, Frances Coutts, daughter of the banker 
2 CONC Thomas Coutts.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1820, he was admitted to Christ Church, Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 July 1824, he married Princess Christine Bonaparte (1798–1847), daughter of Lucien Bonaparte by his first wife, Christine Boyer, and sister of the Princess Gabrielli. They had one son, Paul Amadeus Francis Coutts Stuart, who
2 CONC  died unmarried in 1889.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the Whittington Club and the Literary Association of the Friends of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Whig and subsequently Liberal, he was a passionate advocate of Polish independence,[2] and sympathetic in general to the cause of the Eastern European peoples against Russia. He received Lajos Kossuth in England after his exile 
2 CONC from Hungary. In the election of 1857, Richard Cobden told an anecdote referring to this event:
2 CONT
2 CONT I will tell you what happend with my knowledge -- it is no breach of confidence to say it. When that illustrious Hungarian was expected in England, after his imprisonment in Turkey, my lamented friend Lord Dudley Stuart -- whose d
2 CONC evotion to the cause of these foreign refugees was as unbounded as it was sincere - went down to Southampton to meet Kossuth, and receive him on his arrival. Having to wait a day or two there, and being in the neighbourhood of Bro
2 CONC adlands, where Lord Palmerston lives, he went there and saw the noble lord, and received from him a request to bring Kossuth over to Broadlands, to see him. I remember receiveing a letter from Lord Dudley Stuart, announcing to me 
2 CONC this piece of intelligence with the greatest glee. He was delighted at the opportunity of taking Kossuth over to the Lord Palmerston; and as soon as he arrived he announced to him the pleasing information. To his astonishment he f
2 CONC ound Kossuth would not accept it. He would not go near Lord Palmerston -- (cheers); and I have got a letter from Lord Dudley Stuart, asking me to use all my influence with Kossuth to induce him to go and call upon Lord Palmerston.
2 CONC  He would not do it; and my answer was this, "You may depend upon it, Kossuth knows a great deal more about Lord Palmerston than you do." (Laughter).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT A critic of the Metropolitan Police, he suggested a reduction of the strength of the force in 1853.
2 CONT --------------------
