1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Auguste Charles Joseph de Flahaut de La Billarderie, Comte de Flahaut de La Billarderie (21 April 1785–2 September 1870) was a French general and statesman. He was the lover of Napoleon Bonaparte's step-daughter, H
2 CONC ortense de Beauharnais, Queen of Holland, by whom he had an illegitimate son, Charles Auguste Louis Joseph Demorny, known later as the Duc de Morny.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Paris, the son of Alexandre Sébastien de Flahaut de La Billarderie, Comte de Flahaut de La Billarderie, beheaded at Arras in February 1793, and his wife Adélaïde Filleul, afterwards M.me de Souza-Botelho. Charles de
2 CONC  Flahaut was generally recognized to be the offspring of his mother's liaison with Talleyrand, with whom he was closely connected throughout his life. His mother took him with her into exile in 1792, and they remained abroad until
2 CONC  1798.
2 CONT
2 CONT He entered the army as a volunteer in 1800, and received his commission after the Battle of Marengo. He became aide-de-camp to Murat, and was wounded at the Battle of Landbach in 1805. At Warsaw he met Anne Poniatowski, Countess P
2 CONC otocka, with whom he rapidly became intimate. After the Battle of Friedland he received the Legion of Honour, and returned to Paris in 1807. He served in Spain in 1808, and then in Germany. Meanwhile the Countess Potocka had estab
2 CONC lished herself in Paris, but Charles de Flahaut had by this entered on his liaison with Hortense de Beauharnais, Queen of Holland. The birth of their son was registered in Paris on 21 October 1811 as Charles Auguste Louis Joseph D
2 CONC emorny, known later as the Duc de Morny. Flahaut fought with distinction in the Russian campaign of 1812, and in 1813 became general of brigade, aide-de-camp to the emperor, and, after the Battle of Leipzig, general of division. A
2 CONC fter Napoleon's abdication in 1814 he submitted to the new government, but was placed on the retired list in September. He was assiduous in his attendance on Queen Hortense until the Hundred Days brought him into active service ag
2 CONC ain. A mission to Vienna to secure the return of Marie Louise resulted in failure. He was present at Waterloo, and afterwards sought to place Napoleon II on the throne. He was saved from exile by Talleyrand's influence, but was pl
2 CONC aced under police surveillance. Presently he elected to retire to Germany, and thence to England, where he married in Edinburgh on 20 June 1817 Margaret Mercer Elphinstone (1788–1867), daughter of Admiral George Keith Elphinstone,
2 CONC  1st Viscount Keith, and after the latter's death 2nd Baroness Keith in her own right. The French ambassador opposed the marriage, and Flahaut resigned his commission. His eldest daughter, Emily Jane, married Henry Petty-Fitzmauri
2 CONC ce, 4th Marquess of Lansdowne. His youngest daughter Georgiana Gabrielle de Flahaut, died on 16 July 1907, married on 2 February 1871 the Marquis de Lavalette, who died in 1881.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Flahauts returned to France in 1827, and in 1830 Louis Philippe gave the count the grade of lieutenant-general and made him a peer of France. He remained intimately associated with Talleyrand's policy, and was, for a short tim
2 CONC e in 1831, ambassador at Berlin. He was afterwards attached to the household of the duke of Orleans, and in 1841 was sent as ambassador to Vienna, where he remained until 1848, when he was dismissed and retired from the army. Afte
2 CONC r the coup d'état of 1851 he was again actively employed, and from 1860 to 1862 was ambassador at the court of St James's. He died in Paris on 1 September 1870.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Comte de Flahaut is perhaps better remembered for his exploits in gallantry, and the elegant manners in which he had been carefully trained by his mother, than for his public services, which were not, however, so inconsiderabl
2 CONC e as they have sometimes been represented to be.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Joseph,_Comte_de_Flahaut]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Auguste Charles Joseph de Flahault, Comte de Flahault de la Billardrie''' 
2 CONT * Son of Charles Maurice de Tallyrand-Périgord, Prince of Benevento. 
2 CONT * Married Margaret Mercer Elphinstone, Baroness Keith, daughter of Admiral George Keith Elphinstone, 1st and last Viscount Keith and Jane Mercer, on 20 June 1817.
2 CONT * Died on 2 September 1870.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of French Ambassador to the United Kingdom and French Ambassador to Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Aide-de-Camp to Napoleon.
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the title of Comte de Flahault de la Billardrie.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p3307.htm#i33067
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He had an affair with Hortense de Beauharnais, Queen of Holland, and their son was born in Paris and was registered on 21 October 1811, named Charles Auguste Louis Joseph Demorny. Meanwhile, Flahaut fought well in the 1812 Russian
2 CONC  campaign, and was promoted in 1813 as aide-de-camp to the emperor, general of brigade, and became general of division after the Battle of Leipzig. In 1814, after Napoleon was abdicated, he yielded to the present government, but w
2 CONC as put on the retired list in September. He diligently attended to Queen Hortense but he was brought into active service again when the Hundred Days came. The mission to secure Marie Louise’s return from Vienna failed. He was at W
2 CONC aterloo at that time, and had sought afterwards to give the throne to Napoleon II. He was only spared from exile due to the influence of Talleyrand, but was put under the surveillance of the police. He chose to spend his retiremen
2 CONC t in Germany, and then went to England after a short period of living in Paris. He got married on 20 June 1817 in Edinburgh to Margaret Mercer Elphinstone, who was the daughter of Admiral George Keith Elphinstone, 1st Viscount Kei
2 CONC th, and when her father died, she became in her own right the 2nd Baroness Keith. Charles de Flahaut remained closely connected with Talleyrand's policy, and became an ambassador at Berlin for a short while in 1831. He continued s
2 CONC erving the army but eventually retired. He died on 1 September 1870 in Paris, France.
