1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Josef Anton Joseph Baptist Erzherzog von Österreich was a member of the House of Habsburg-Lorraine. 
2 CONT *Erzherzog von Österreich (styled as HI&RH Archduke of Austria).
2 CONT *Governor and Captain-General of Hungary in 1795.
2 CONT *Palatine of Hungary in 1796.
2 CONT *General Field Marshal in the service of the Austrian Army
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Links:'''
2 CONT *[http://www.thepeerage.com/p10133.htm#i101329 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/D/per_page.php?id=9870 Geneall]
2 CONT *'''Wikipedia:''' [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archduke_Joseph,_Palatine_of_Hungary English ] [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Anton_Johann_von_%C3%96sterreich Deutsch ]  [http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C3%B3zsef_n%C3%A1dor M
2 CONC agyar]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Német-római császári herceg, osztrák főherceg, magyar királyi herceg, császári-királyi tábornagy (k.k. Generalfeldmarschall), 1796-tól haláláig Magyarország nádora, a Habsburg-ház ún. magyar vagy nádori ágának megalapítója.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archduke Joseph Anton of Austria (József nádor), Palatine of Hungary, is considered one of the principal founders of modern Budapest, because during his tenure the systematic transformation of Pest from a provincial trading town i
2 CONC nto an important administrative and cultural center began. His role was primarily organizational and political: he supported and directed projects that changed the character of the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the beginning of the nineteenth century Pest was a city with chaotic development. Its streets were narrow and winding, neighborhoods had grown without a unified plan, and floods of the Danube frequently caused damage to buildin
2 CONC gs. At the initiative of the Palatine, a comprehensive urban regulation plan for the city was prepared. The architect János Hild designed a project that proposed straightening and widening streets, creating a more regular urban st
2 CONC ructure, forming new public squares, and gradually replacing old wooden buildings with stone construction.
2 CONT To implement these ideas, a special institution was established in 1808, the Commission for the Beautification and Regulation of Pest (Szépítő Bizottság). Its leadership was entrusted to the Palatine Joseph. The commission approve
2 CONC d building projects, supervised urban planning, regulated the architectural appearance of new structures, and encouraged the construction of public buildings. This represented the first systematic attempt at modern urban planning 
2 CONC in Hungary.
2 CONT At the same time, the Palatine actively supported cultural and scientific institutions. With his patronage museums, educational institutions, theaters, and scientific societies developed, and Pest gradually became the intellectual
2 CONC  center of the country. Urban infrastructure also improved: the embankments of the Danube began to take shape, the road network developed, and new parks and public spaces were created.
2 CONT Archduke Joseph lived in Hungary for most of his life and enjoyed considerable respect among the Hungarian population. He was often called “the most Hungarian of the Habsburgs.” For this reason, Hungarian historical memory regards
2 CONC  him as one of the key figures who laid the foundations for the future development of Budapest. In recognition of this role, the square József nádor tér in Budapest bears his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Alcsut'''
2 CONT The estate of Alcsút came into the possession of Archduke Joseph Anton of Habsburg-Lorraine (József nádor, Palatine of Hungary) in 1819, and from that moment the history of what later became one of the most notable aristocratic es
2 CONC tates in Hungary began. When the Palatine acquired Alcsút, he did not receive a ready-made residence but rather a landscape that had to be created almost from the beginning. The surrounding land was relatively poor and sandy, and 
2 CONC in places not very fertile. This circumstance, however, seems to have inspired him even more. Joseph approached the estate not merely as the owner of a noble property but with genuine personal interest and enthusiasm. Alcsút becam
2 CONC e for him not only a country residence but also a place where he could realize his ideas about the harmonious combination of nature, agriculture, architecture, and cultivated landscape. Shortly after acquiring the estate, he commi
2 CONC ssioned the design of a palace from the distinguished Hungarian architect Mihály Pollack, and during the 1820s a new archducal residence was gradually built; the family took possession of the palace in 1827. Yet the creation of th
2 CONC e surrounding park was no less important to the Palatine than the construction of the palace itself. This was closely connected to his personal interest in botany, gardening, and the acclimatization of plants. Joseph devoted much 
2 CONC attention to planting trees and introducing rare species, invited gardeners and botanical specialists, and brought new varieties of plants from different parts of Europe. His aim was not merely to adorn the residence but to transf
2 CONC orm the area into a model estate with a carefully designed landscape park in the English style. Contemporary accounts noted his persistence in improving the land: the poorer and less fertile the soil appeared, the more determined 
2 CONC he was to cultivate and enrich it. Over time the former barren land was transformed into one of the richest parks in Hungary, containing a remarkable variety of trees and shrubs and serving not only as a place of recreation but al
2 CONC so as a kind of botanical experiment. For this reason, the origin of today’s Alcsút Arboretum is directly connected with the work of the Palatine: he created the estate and its park, and although the palace itself did not survive,
2 CONC  the park endured and later became an important botanical and historical site. After Joseph’s death the estate remained in the possession of his descendants, who continued to develop it. In the 1880s, during the time of the next g
2 CONC eneration, the former stable building near the main palace was converted into a chapel. According to tradition, the idea arose after the bishop of Székesfehérvár visited the estate and remarked that the elegant classical building 
2 CONC with its columns could easily serve as a chapel, adding that even the stable where the Savior of mankind was born had not been more beautiful. Following this remark, Archduke Joseph Karl commissioned the architect Ferenc Storno to
2 CONC  redesign the building, and Storno’s plans later received a gold medal at a national exhibition. The interior decoration of the chapel involved several prominent Hungarian artists and craftsmen. Storno designed the mosaic floor an
2 CONC d window frames, the ceiling was adorned with frescoes, the altarpiece and some other paintings were created by members of the archducal family, the stained-glass windows were produced by Miksa Róth, the organ was built in the ren
2 CONC owned Angster workshop in Pécs, and the ceiling frescoes were painted by Károly Lotz. Thus Alcsút became not only a residence but also an important artistic and cultural center. During the Second World War the palace suffered heav
2 CONC y damage, and in 1944 a fire also affected the chapel. In 1951 the demolition of the palace complex was ordered. The palace itself, together with the palm house, orangery, glasshouses, bathhouse, stables, and gloriette, was destro
2 CONC yed. Only a few structures survived, including the palace portico, the chapel, the riding hall, several service buildings, and the so-called Babaház (Doll House). The park, however, was preserved because of its natural and histori
2 CONC cal value. As a result, the former palace park created under the patronage of the Palatine gradually developed into the present-day Alcsút Arboretum, which continues to exist today. In this way Alcsút preserves the memory of sever
2 CONC al aspects of Joseph’s life: as a statesman and member of the Habsburg dynasty, as the creator of a model estate, and as a man deeply devoted to nature, botany, and landscape cultivation, leaving a lasting mark not only on Hungari
2 CONC an political history but also on its cultural and environmental heritage.
