2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT On 4 November 1824, she married Franz Karl, Archduke of Austria. They had six children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her ambition to place her oldest son on the Austrian throne was a constant theme in Austrian politics. At the time she was called "the only man at court". During the Revolution of 1848, she persuaded her somewhat feeble-minded hus
2 CONC band to give up his rights to the throne in favour of Franz Joseph, her son. After Franz Joseph's accession, Sophie became the power behind the throne. Historically, Sophie is remembered for her extremely controlling relationship 
2 CONC with Franz Joseph's famed wife Sisi, who was also her niece.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie kept a detailed diary most of her life which reveals much about Austrian court life. She was deeply affected in 1867 by the execution in Mexico of her second son Maximilian. She never recovered from that shock, and withdrew
2 CONC  from public life. She died of a brain tumor in 1872.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also noted for her close relationship with Napoleon II, who lived at the Austrian Court as the Duke of Reichstadt. There were rumors of a sexual affair between them. There was even suspicion that Maximilian, born two weeks
2 CONC  before Reichstadt's death in 1832, was actually his child. These claims were never verified, but it is certain that they were very good friends and that his death affected her very much. She is said to have turned into the cold, 
2 CONC ambitious woman described in fiction after he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Franz Joseph 18 August 1830 21 November 1916 succeeded as Emperor of Austria married his first cousin Elisabeth, Duchess in Bavaria, and had issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximilian 6 July 1832 19 June 1867 proclaimed Emperor of Mexico  executed by a firing squad  married Charlotte, Princess of Belgium, and had no issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Karl Ludwig 30 July 1833 19 May 1896 married 1) his first cousin Margaretha, Princess of and Duchess in Saxony, (1840–1858) from 1856 to 1858, no issue, married 2) to Maria Annunziata, Princess of the Two-Sicilies (1843–1871) from
2 CONC  1862 to 1871, had issue (three sons and one daughter) and married 3) to Maria Theresia, Infanta of Portugal, (1855–1944), from 1873 to 1899, had issue (two daughters). He was the father of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, whose assassin
2 CONC ation in 1914 sparked World War I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Anna 27 October 1835 5 February 1840 died in childhood 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stillborn son 24 October 1840 24 October 1840  
2 CONT
2 CONT Ludwig Viktor 15 May 1842 18 January 1919 died unmarried 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Princess Sophie of Bavaria was born to King Maximilian I Joseph of Bavaria and his second wife Caroline of Baden. She was the identical twin sister of Princess Maria Anna of Bavaria, Queen of Saxony as wife of Frederick Augustus I
2 CONC I of Saxony. Her eldest son Franz Joseph reigned as Emperor of Austria, and King of Hungary; her second son Maximilian reigned as Emperor of Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 November 1824, she married Franz Karl, Archduke of Austria. Her paternal half-sister, Caroline Augusta of Bavaria, had married the groom's widowed father, Francis II, in 1816. Sophie and Franz Karl had six children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her ambition to place her oldest son on the Austrian throne was a constant theme in Austrian politics. At the time she was called "the only man at court". During the Revolution of 1848, she persuaded her somewhat feeble-minded hus
2 CONC band to give up his rights to the throne in favour of their son Franz Joseph. After Franz Joseph's accession, Sophie became the power behind the throne. Historically, Sophie is remembered for her extremely controlling relationship
2 CONC  with Franz Joseph's wife Sisi, who was also her niece.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie kept a detailed diary most of her life, which reveals much about Austrian court life. She was deeply affected in 1867 by the execution in Mexico of her second son Maximilian. She never recovered from that shock, and withdre
2 CONC w from public life. She died of a brain tumor in 1872.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also noted for her close relationship with Napoleon II, who lived at the Austrian Court as the Duke of Reichstadt. There were rumors of a sexual affair between them. There was even suspicion that Maximilian, born two weeks
2 CONC  before Reichstadt's death in 1832, was actually his child. These claims were never verified, but it is certain that they were very good friends and that his death affected her very much. She is said to have turned into the cold, 
2 CONC ambitious woman described in fiction after he died.
2 CONT
