1 NOTE {geni:about_me} François Tobias Dos
2 CONT Louise Aimée Julie Leclerc (June 19, 1782 – December 17, 1868) was born during one of France's most turbulent periods. She was a true product of her time. Immersed in new ideologies from birth, she became a fervent admirer of Paul
2 CONC ine Léon and Claire Lacombe, who frequently visited her parents' estate in Pontoise with her cousin Jean-Théophile Victor Leclerc, founder of the Enragés.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aimée was only 11 years old when Pauline Léon and Claire Lacombe co-founded the Société des Républicaines Révolutionnaires (Society of Revolutionary Republicans) in February 1793.
2 CONT
2 CONT She looked older than she was. For this reason, she often visited them with Jean-Théophile Victor Leclerc at the club's headquarters in Paris. Always eager to please others, she enjoyed helping collect the club's entrance fees. Sh
2 CONC e performed her duties so skillfully that she was nicknamed "Henriette" (meaning "guardian of the house").
2 CONT
2 CONT The Société des Républicaines Révolutionnaires (with 200 exclusively female members) was generally considered a kind of "feminist" branch of Jean Théophile Victor Leclerc Enragés. The club was banned the following year by Robespie
2 CONC rre. Pauline Léon, Claire Lacombe, and most of the members of the Société, including Henriette (Aimée) Leclerc, who were present at the club, were arrested in April 1794. They were imprisoned in Luxembourg Prison, where they remai
2 CONC ned until August 19, 1794.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parents decided to send Aimée, with her striking beauty, to Madame Campan's renowned institution in Saint-Germain, where she would meet Hortense de Beauharnais and Caroline Bonaparte (Napoleon's sister).
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoleon married Rose de Beauharnais (the future Empress Josephine) on October 3, 1796, apparently unaware that Aimée, whom he affectionately called his "Madame Deux," had returned to her parents' home in Pontoise. Aimée was only 
2 CONC 14 years old when she gave birth to a son, allegedly fathered by Napoleon. She named him François Tobias Deux and remained in Pontoise with her parents. They raised François Tobias as their own son.
2 CONT
2 CONT A frequent visitor to Pontoise was Laetitia Bonaparte, Madame Mère, who was particularly concerned for the well-being of Aimée and the baby.
2 CONT
2 CONT Madame Mère decided to find a suitable husband for Aimée. She soon set her sights on Louis Nicholas Davout, Duke of Auerstadt and Prince of Eckmühl, a descendant of a noble family whose origins dated back to the 13th century. He s
2 CONC tudied at the military academy in Auxerre and later at the École Militaire in Paris. Davout had already divorced his first wife, Marie-Nicolle Adelaide (De Seguenot was her maiden name), in January 1794. Aimée and Davout married i
2 CONC n November 1801. They appear to have been a happy couple. They had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Paul (1802–1803)
2 CONT 2. Josephine (1804–1805)
2 CONT 3. Antoinette Josephine (1805–August 19, 1821)
2 CONT 4. Adèle Napoléonie (June 1, 1807–June 21, 1885)
2 CONT 5. Napoléon (March 1809–1810)
2 CONT 6. Jules (1809) (died in infancy)
2 CONT 7. Napoléon Louis (January 6, 1811–August 13, 1853)
2 CONT 8. Jules (1812–1813)
2 CONT 9. Louise Adélaïde (July 8, 1815–October 6, 1892)
2 CONT
2 CONT As you can see, many of the children did not survive infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT François Tobias Deux, however, remained a threat to Josephine, who feared that the boy would one day succeed Napoleon as emperor instead of her daughter Hortense's son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josephine began to devise all sorts of schemes, which led Madame Mère herself to convince Napoleon to send François Tobias to the Dutch East Indies to be educated and, eventually, become king. The East Indies had become part of th
2 CONC e French empire when Napoleon conquered Holland and all its territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT François Tobias was only eight years old when he embarked on the long journey to Batavia. He was placed in the care of the Dusantier family, a high-ranking family of French descent. They immediately began the important task of pre
2 CONC paring him for his future. The Dusantier family presented him to all the sultans, who were immediately captivated by the handsome boy. It was Susuhunan (Sultan) Paku Buono IV of Solo [http://www.royalark.net/Indonesia/solo.htm] hi
2 CONC mself who opened his home to François, where he was treated as one of the family. There he received the Javanese name: Bra Kedaton.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1811, Java came under the control of Lord Minto, Governor-General of India, who, after the surrender, appointed Thomas Stamford Raffles Lieutenant Governor. Many French people left Java, but François Tobias and the Dusantiers (
2 CONC also known as the Dezentjé family) felt the need to stay. They enjoyed the special protection of the Susuhunan (Sultan) in Solo.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1816, François Tobias married Leonara Caroline Dusantier, the young girl who had treated him so kindly upon his arrival in Batavia. Sadly, she passed away.
