3 CITY Fredericksburg
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 OCT 1846
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fredericksburg
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 TITL Col.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Several of Colonel Henry Willis’ (one of the founders of Fredericksburg) descendants rest on what is known as “Willis Hill”.
2 CONT
2 CONT This cemetery covers approximately one-half acre; is enclosed with a brick wall and substantial iron gate; a carpet of periwinkle, several gnarled old trees –honeysuckle rapidly making its way over them – nature truly precominatin
2 CONC g.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Willis’ grave is marked by a stone with the following inscription:
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Byrd C. Willis Born August 29, 1781 Died October 1st, 1846
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Willis’ father was Lewis Willis and his mother Anne Carter of Cleve. She was married three times but has only the one son, Byrd C. Willis.  They hada child whom they named Charles Lewis, but he only lived to the age of thr
2 CONC eeyears, and at his death they added Charles Lewis to Colonel Byrd Willis’ name,making it Byrd Charles Lewis.  Colonel Willis became weary of having such a long name, so imply called himself Byrd C. Willis, and his father never fo
2 CONC rgave him for it.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was very proud of being a descendent of the Byrd’s of Westover, and was determined the Colonel Willis should retain the name Byrd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Byrd C. Willis’ father was rather unusually name, being called Lewis to commemorate the memory of his mother’s former husband, “Lewis”.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Willis’ father, Lewis Willis, was left an orphan at the age of twelve years.  The estate was well cared for, but his education was very much neglected.  He was a school-mate of George Washington, being two years older than
2 CONC  George.  Lewis Willis often spoke of how diligent George Washington was at school; while most of the boys would be playing, George would be busy cyphering.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Byrd C. Willis was very much petted, in fact more than he cared for, and as he became older he felt that his mother treated him as if he were a small child.  His father was rather stern and severe.  He would say to the sch
2 CONC ool master “I am most unfortunate, none of my children love their books; they are a stiff necked, perverse generation, and nothing but flogging will do for them.”  The school masters would very often take his advice, and for that 
2 CONC reason Colonel Willis was not fond of school.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1800 Colonel Willis married at the age of nineteen, Mary Willis Lewis, daughter of George Lewis of Marmion, nephew of General George Washington.  From infancy the parents had planned this match, and the day it was consummated i
2 CONC t was one of the happiest for Mrs. Willis, the mother of Byrd C. Willis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Willis and his bride at first made their home in Orange, near the Court House, and later they came to “Willis Hill”.  He paid little attention to the management of the plantation, spending his time fox hunting, racing, and
2 CONC  attending parties; and but from the profits from the races, and the sale of fire wood, they would have run through their property long before they did.  Things continued to get worse, and finally in 1825, he sold out, paid off an
2 CONC d removed to Florida.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following children were born to Colonel Willis and his wife Mary, (Willis Lewis) Willis:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis Willis; Catherine Dangerfield Willis; John Willis; George Willis; Mary Byrd Willis; and Ellen A. Willis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine Willis married at a very early age Atchison Gray, son of John Gray of “Travelers Rest”.  Her husband died in less than twelve months after their marriage, and their child born after his death, died also.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine, a very young widow, went with her father and mother to Florida, where she met Achilles Murat, ex-Prince of Naples and nephew of Napoleon Bonaparte.  She soon married him and they made their home sixteen miles from Talla
2 CONC hassee. Before Colonel Willis would give his consent to the marriage he got the approval of the Murat and Bonaparte families.  At first they did countenance the marriage on account of Catherine Willis Gray being an American widow,
2 CONC  but when they realized that the charming young lady was a great niece of General Washington, they readily acceded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Byrd Willis, his wife and several of their children, stayed with Joseph Bonaparte, ex-King of Spain, at his magnificent villa near Bordentown, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Library of Virginia Digital Collection
2 CONT
2 CONT LVA Titled Files: Survey Report, Willis cemetery: 1937 July 28 Research made by Sue K. Gordon
2 CONT
2 CONT Cemetery Location:  On Marye Heights or Willis Hill. 1 mile west of Fredericksburg, Va., 500 feet north from the National Cemetery.  This burying ground is reached through the north gate of the National Cemetery. Spotsylvania Coun
2 CONC ty.   https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/tree/28142865/media/db434986-02d5-4495-87e7-d3c17ef542b9?o_xid=57109&o_lid=57109&o_sch=Email+Programs
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Served in the War of 1812. Daughter Mary Byrd Willis,married Comm.Alexander James Dallas;Ellen Attaway Willis,married William H.Brockenbrough of Florida and daughter,Ann Carter (Catherine)married Thomas H.Botts,ESQ on October 24,1
2 CONC 822.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/13413815/byrd-charles_lewis-willis Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 27 2019, 14:35:20 UTC''
