1 NAME William "of Rossgarrow" /Patterson/ Sr
2 GIVN William "of Rossgarrow"
2 NSFX Sr
2 _MARNM Patterson
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 4 JAN 1694
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rossgarrow is a townland near Millford, in Tullyfern Parish, barony of Kilmacrenan, diocese of Raphoe, at the southern end of Fanad Peninsula, in northern County Donegal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scots Patterson and Paterson surnames are both patronymics from Pate, a shortened version of the first name Patrick, the earlier Gaelic version of which had been MacPhadraig.  One version of the alternate spellings of the surn
2 CONC ame is that Queen Elizabeth chose to mark those who left the Catholic faith for the Church of England by requiring those Protestant Patersons to adopt a second 't' in their name; it is unknown if this is accurate although Paterson
2 CONC  is the predominate spelling of the name in Scotland, and William Patterson of Baltimore, MD, in his will, gave autobiographical detail of his family being of the "Episcopal Church, the official church of Ireland", giving possible
2 CONC  credence to this folklore.
2 CONT
2 CONT POSSIBLY, William was the son of John and Ann (Knox) Patterson, later of Hillsborough, County Down, Ireland, and grandson of John and Helen/Ellen (Gay) Patterson, although this is as yet unverified.  In FamilySearch.org, William i
2 CONC s denoted to have been born 04Jan1694/5 at Rossgarrow, Milford, County Donegal to "John Robert and Joanna (Knox) Patterson".  As a point of interest, the Knox family had been prominate landowners in that region in the 1600s.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Son's (William) autobiographical portion of his will denotes his father as having been a farmer with a large family.
