1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was a charter member, and subsequently treasurer (1788) of Walmsley Lodge No. 14 at Pictou, created 07Dec1785 by the Provincial Grand Lodge in Halifax. He was made a Mason in Lodge No. 2 (Moderns) at Philadelphi
2 CONC a, October 14th, 1758.(Old Masonic Lodges in Pennsylvania Vol. 1, Page 76).
2 CONT ---------------------------
2 CONT THERE IS SUBSTANTIAL DOUBT THAT THIS PERSON'S MOTHER COULD BE HANNAH MERRIMOON PATTERSON, based only on his brother's (William) documented testimony that his mother was Elizabeth (Peoples) Patterson of County Donegal, Ireland.
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT From the Dictionary of Canadian Biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT PATTERSON, ROBERT, surveyor, merchant, office holder, and militia officer; b. 1732 in Renfrew, Scotland; d. 30 Sept. 1808 in Pictou, N.S.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his emigration to America, Robert Patterson worked for some time as a pedlar, and prior to 1763 apparently was also a sutler with the British army. In 1767 he was living at Cross Roads (Churchville) in Maryland. When the
2 CONC  Philadelphia Company, an association of American land developers, organized a group of settlers to go to its newly acquired land grant on the northern coast of Nova Scotia, Patterson was selected as the expedition’s surveyor. In 
2 CONC May 1767 Patterson, his wife, and their five children sailed on the Betsey with John Harris's and several other families. He soon became involved in trading ventures at the new settlement of Pictou, supplying goods to both Indians
2 CONC  and settlers in return for furs, timber, and farm produce. Professional fees from surveying and business profits soon made him relatively prosperous; his sons were educated by hired tutors, and he had the first frame-house in the
2 CONC  settlement while the other residents lived in rude cabins. When James McGregor, pioneer missionary to the Highland settlers in the area, preached his first sermon at Pictou in 1786, he did so in Patterson’s barn, where rough benc
2 CONC hes had been improvised.
2 CONT
2 CONT An educated man, with influential friends among the Scottish "merchantocracy" that played such a vital part in the North Atlantic trade, Patterson shortly became the leading man in the settlement. As a justice of the peace (appoin
2 CONC ted 6 April 1774) and known as Squire Patterson to distinguish him from John "Deacon" Patterson, he was the "venerable settler [who] presided over the others . . . while they, with confidence . . . yielded in most cases obedience 
2 CONC to his counsels. . . ." He was considered "a sort of factotum for all the settlers, even celebrating their marriages."
2 CONT
2 CONT During the American revolution Patterson, unlike most of the other settlers from America, favoured the British side. In his enthusiasm he attempted to arrest some of the disloyal inhabitants, and was warned off by threats of murde
2 CONC r. When trying to reach Halifax for copies of the oath of allegiance which Governor Francis Legge had ordered everyone in the province to take, he was forced to return home by an armed American settler at Truro. Possibly Patterson
2 CONC  had not spent enough time in the American colonies to be influenced by the revolutionary movement. It is more likely, however, that as a far-sighted businessman he perceived the opportunities for profitable trade if the colony re
2 CONC mained in Britain’s good graces, and he was also well aware that the Highland Scots, the majority of Pictou’s inhabitants, were strongly pro-British. In recognition of his services he was made a captain of militia in 1783.
