1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henriette_von_Oberkirch
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Henriette von Oberkirch (* 5. July 1754 in Schweighouse (German: Schweighausen) at Thann in Alsace; † 10. June 1803 in Strasbourg), better known than Baronne d’Oberkirch, was born Waldner von Freundstein. Her parents were Freiherr
2 CONC , later Graf, Franz Ludwig Waldner von Freundstein (1710 – 1788), master too Schmieheim, and his wife Wilhelmine Auguste von Berckheim to Rappoltsweiler.
2 CONT
2 CONT After marrying Siegfried von Oberkirch in 1776 and the birth of the only child Marie Philippine, she accompanied her friend, princess Sophie Dorothee of Württemberg, the later Russian tsarina Maria Fyodorovna, on a journey through
2 CONC  Europe. She also came to the yard Louis XVI and Marie-Antoinettes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among other things, she was with Jakob Michael Reinhold Lenz friends who fell in love with her. In 1776 he described in a letter to Goethe his (unrequested) feelings for her: Nothing to compare to a suffering of the kind when it w
2 CONC as going on in the world.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her memoirs, published posthumously by her grandson Graf Léon de Montbrison, are considered an excellent source – despite certain concerns about authenticity –. In them you can read like in the idyll of the ducal estate (Etupes at
2 CONC  Mömpelgard) The spirit and conviviality of the ending Ancien Régime came to life for the last time in all their grace.  The baroness Oberkirch, writes Robert Uhland, captured the enchanting atmosphere of the courtyard and life at
2 CONC  the Mömpelgarder Hof is vividly described.[2nd]
2 CONT
2 CONT A three-volume English edition (Memoirs) appeared in London in 1852, a two-volume one in Paris in 1853. Further editions followed, because the memoirs were received with great interest.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2003 was on the occasion of their 200th On the day of her death, her memory was celebrated in Schweighouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
