1 NAME Susan /Starkey/
2 GIVN Susan
2 SURN Starkey
2 _MARNM Coutts
2 NICK Elizabeth
2 NICK Starkie
2 NICK Susannah
2 NICK Betty
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 JUN 1743
2 ADDR
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 JAN 1815
1 OCCU Servant
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} thepeerage.com names her Susan Starkie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage record says Susannah Starkie.
2 CONT
2 CONT In http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AG19070311.2.2&srpos=3&e=-------10--1----0frederick+john+coutts-- she is Betty Starky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Betty's father was a husbandman in Lancashire who raised a large family on a very small farm.  Betty was employed as a housemaid by Thomas Coutts' elder brother.  http://books.google.co.nz/books?id=4-wEAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA2&lpg=PA2&dq=%
2 CONC 22betty+starky%22+coutts+housemaid&source=bl&ots=ODInTMY-_b&sig=SaOhtlfpkf3xN9JT7xj5koZH73w&hl=en&sa=X&ei=nmikT8izGsafiAeo3u2zAw&ved=0CE0Q6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=%22betty%20starky%22%20coutts%20housemaid&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT Had four sons who died in infancy and three daughters
2 CONT
2 CONT Her funeral was 14 Jan 1815 according to some sources, i.e, four days before her husband remarried.  Other sources say she died 4 Jan 1814.
2 CONT
2 CONT Extract from "The life of Thomas Coutts, banker" .. (1920):
2 CONT
2 CONT "There is no mystery concerning the origin of the first Mrs Coutts. She was a native of the town or neighbourhood  of Preston in Lancashire (the daughter of a husbandman or small farmer), and before she came up to London she had b
2 CONC een in the service of the Misses Grodwell, "three ancient maiden Aunts" of a certain Mrs Mary Assheton of Preston, a gentlewoman who was in correspondence with Thomas Coutts as late as December 21, 182 1.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1833 Coutt's second wife, afterwards Duchess of St Albans, made inquiries through her lawyers, the Messrs Parkinson of Lincoln's Inn Fields, and it was ascertained that the first Mrs Coutts was the elder of two sisters, Catheri
2 CONC ne and Jane, who married in their own rank of life, and of one brother, Captain Nicholas Starkie (of whom more hereafter), who died in 1809. I do not know if anything came of these inquiries, but it is improbable that the Duchess 
2 CONC was advised to recognise or  make any allowance to the grand-nephews and grand-nieces of her predecessor. In his lifetime Thomas Coutts purchased a small house at Walham Green for his brother-in-law's use, and at his death present
2 CONC ed it to the widow and daughter. He also paid small annuities to one of his wife's sisters (a Mrs Barnes), and to his wife's niece, Miss Jane Starkie, and these annuities he directed the second Mrs Coutts to continue after his dea
2 CONC th. There is nothing to show when Mrs Barnes died, but for nine or ten years the Duchess allowed Miss Starkie £100 a year, and occasionally called upon her. It was at her death in 1833 that she was prompted to inquire if anything 
2 CONC more ought to be done for the family. One would suppose that the obligation, if any, to befriend these humble kinsfolk rested rather upon the daughters than the widow of Thomas Coutts, but it is highly improbable that their parent
2 CONC s ever spoke of them, or that the younger generation knew of their existence."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The banking business in London was in the hands of James and Thomas Coutts, sons of John Coutts. From the death of his brother in 1778, Thomas, as surviving partner, became sole head of the firm; and under his direction the bankin
2 CONC g house rose to the highest distinction. His ambition was to establish his character as a man of business and to make a fortune; and he lived to succeed in this aim and long to enjoy his reputation and wealth. A gentleman in manne
2 CONC rs, hospitable and benevolent, he counted amongst his friends some of the literary men and the best actors of his day. Of the enormous wealth which came into his hands he made munificent use.
2 CONT
2 CONT His private life was not without its romantic elements. Soon after his settlement in London he married Elizabeth Starkey, a young woman of humble origin, who was in attendance on the daughter of his brother James. They lived happi
2 CONC ly together, and had three daughters: Susan, married in 1796 to the 3rd Earl of Guilford; Frances, married in 1800 to John, 1st Marquess of Bute; and Sophia, married in 1793 to Sir Francis Burdett. Mrs Coutts died in 1815. Soon af
2 CONC ter her husband married the popular actress, Harriot Mellon, leaving to her the whole of his immense fortune. He died in London on 24 February 1822.
