1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AG19070311.2.2&srpos=3&e=-------10--1----0frederick+john+coutts-- The Romantic Story of Couts's Bank - A Great Banker Who Married a Housemaid], p
2 CONC rinted in Ashburton Guardian, Volume XXVIII, Issue 7123, 11 March 1907, Page 1<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [http://www.thepeerage.com/p1290.htm#i12892 The Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Thomas Coutts''' <br/>
2 CONT * Married, '''firstly,'''-  '''Susan Starkie'''
2 CONT * Married, '''secondly''' -  '''Harriet Mellon''', daughter of Matthew Mellon.
2 CONT * Died before 1827.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a banker at The Strand, London, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children of Thomas Coutts and Susan Starkie'''
2 CONT * Frances Coutts d. 12 Nov 1832
2 CONT * Sophia Coutts d. 12 Jan 1844
2 CONT * Susan Coutts d. 24 Sep 1837
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thomas Coutts (7 September 1735 – 24 February 1822) was an Anglo-Scottish banker who was the founder of the banking house of Coutts & Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the fourth son of John Coutts (1699–1751), who carried on business in Edinburgh as a corn factor and negotiator of bills of exchange, and who in 1742 was elected lord provost of the city. The family was originally of Montro
2 CONC se, but about 1696 one of its members had settled at Edinburgh, where in due course Thomas received his education at the Royal High School. Soon after the death of John Coutts the business was divided into two branches, one carrie
2 CONC d on in Edinburgh, the other in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT The banking business in London was in the hands of James and Thomas Coutts, sons of John Coutts. From the death of his brother in 1778, Thomas, as surviving partner, became sole head of the firm; and under his direction the bankin
2 CONC g house rose to the highest distinction. His ambition was to establish his character as a man of business and to make a fortune; and he lived to succeed in this aim and long to enjoy his reputation and wealth. A gentleman in manne
2 CONC rs, hospitable and benevolent, he counted amongst his friends some of the literary men and the best actors of his day. Of the enormous wealth which came into his hands he made munificent use.
2 CONT
2 CONT His private life was not without its romantic elements. Soon after his settlement in London he married Elizabeth Starkey, a young woman of humble origin, who was in attendance on the daughter of his brother James. They lived happi
2 CONC ly together, and had three daughters: Susan, married in 1796 to the 3rd Earl of Guilford; Frances, married in 1800 to John, 1st Marquess of Bute; and Sophia, married in 1793 to Sir Francis Burdett. Mrs Coutts died in 1815. Soon af
2 CONC ter her husband married the popular actress, Harriot Mellon, leaving to her the whole of his immense fortune. He died in London on 24 February 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1827, his widow married the 9th duke of St Albans. She died ten years later, bequeathing her property to Angela, youngest daughter of Sir Francis Burdett, who then assumed the additional name and arms of Coutts. In 1871, this l
2 CONC ady was created Baroness Burdett-Coutts.
2 CONT --------------------
