1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Spears Married to 
2 CONT #Patience Cotton in 1723, had Clare and Patience Cotton Spears
2 CONT #Elizabeth Warren, had William, Apsele and John Spears. ?? [not named in will]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT The sister of Mary (Warren) Smithwick, Elizabeth Spier, remembered Samuel and Mary (Warren) Smithwick’s children in her will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Warren Speir had no children and left her entire estate to her nephews and nieces.    She named her brother, Joseph Warren and sister Mary Warren Smithwick as well as many of their children.    This is the only document 
2 CONC that provides the name of the children and some grandchildren of Samuel Smithwick and Mary Warren.   Many Smithwick descendants living the eastern North Carolina descend from Samuel and Mary (Warren) Smithwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://wc.rootsweb.com/trees/246464/I21559/-/individual
2 CONT
2 CONT Abstract of John Speir's will January 8, 1761; Pro. June Ct. 1767 Wife: Elizabeth (negro boy and other chattels). Daughters: Cleare Hardee ("all my lands in North Carolina"), Patience Jeffreys, Apsele Holland. Executors: John and 
2 CONC William Speir (brothers)? and Alexander Stewart. Wit: Wm. Speir, Alexr. Stewart, John Speir, Junr. Clerk of Ct. Amb Cox Bayley. This appears to be the will of his '''widow Elizabeth Speir of Pitt County. October 9, 1773 Pro. March
2 CONC  23, 1774'''. Nephews: Joseph Warren (two negroes), William Smydick [SIC: Smithwick], Marke Mesell (three negroes) Other legatees: Mary Smydick, John Smydick, Edmond Smydick, Famaler Smydick, Mary Meesel, Elizabeth Warren, Joseph 
2 CONC Warren, James and Ma Inscribed on tomb: Death at a defiance we but slightly fear. He brings his terors as he draws more near; >From stately palaces we must be drawn To lie benighted in the tomb alone. Wise then's the man who labor
2 CONC s to secure His passage safe and his reception sure. In S. V. E. Here lies the body of Capt. John Spier, born in Virginia, Sept. 25th, 1693 and departed this life August 23rd, 1764. AEtatis 71. The above inscription is on a tomb s
2 CONC tone on the bank of Tar River, two miles below Greenville. The grave is marked by a brown stone tomb with a slate slab let in, upon which is the inscription. This tomb stands apparently alone. If there are others near, no trace of
2 CONC  them can be seen. It throws some light upon the first residents of this section. It is said that some of the descendants of this man still live in this community. (From Washington Progress, Tuesday 20 Dec 1892. page 3, col 2.) I 
2 CONC found it in NCGen Soc Journal May 1996 issue where item had been submitted by John H. Odem, III, Rt. 1, Box 330, Pinetown, NC, 27865
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/photos/artifacts/19830735
2 CONT *https://studylib.net/doc/7673619/hugh-smithwick-descendants
2 CONT *https://jordantree.tripod.com/william_swaine.htm#Samuel%20SMITHWICK
2 CONT *http://johnrosssr.com/Smithwick.pdf page 8. Lists as 8th child of Edward Smithwick and Elizabeth. Page 35 (No. 11) lists Samuel as possibly son of Africa. Does not list Mary Swaine as a 1st wife and only the Mary Warren children.
2 CONT *https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Spears-715
2 CONT * http://www.palmspringsbum.org/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I98413&tree=legends
