1 TITL Major General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for MARYLAND with the rank of MAJOR GENERAL.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A105737  DAR Ancestor # A105737]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gen Samuel Smith was in Gen Anthony Wayne's regiment at Valley Forge. He was in the battles of Trenton, Princeton, Monmouth, Germantown, and was present at Cornwallis' surrender. He was one of the original members of the Cincinnat
2 CONC i.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Smith_(Maryland%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Smith (July 27, 1752 – April 22, 1839) was a United States Senator and Representative from Maryland, a mayor of Baltimore, Maryland, and a general in the Maryland militia. He was the brother of cabinet secretary Robert Smit
2 CONC h.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Carlisle, Pennsylvania, Smith moved with his family to Baltimore, Maryland, in 1759. He attended a private academy, and engaged in mercantile pursuits until the American Revolutionary War, at which time he served as captai
2 CONC n, major, and lieutenant colonel in the Continental Army. After the war, Smith engaged in the shipping business.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1790 to 1792, Smith was a member of the Maryland House of Delegates. At the time of the threatened war with France in 1794, he was appointed brigadier general of the Maryland militia and commanded Maryland’s quota during the 
2 CONC Whiskey Rebellion. Smith served as a major general of Maryland militia during the War of 1812, and commanded the defenses of Baltimore during the Battle of Baltimore and Fort McHenry in 1814. The American victory there can largely
2 CONC  be attributed to Smith's preparation for the British invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith entered into national politics when he was elected to the Third United States Congress, serving from March 4, 1793, until March 3, 1803. As a Congressman, Smith served as chairman of the U.S. House Committee on Commerce and 
2 CONC Manufactures (Fifth through Seventh Congresses). Smith entered into the Senate election in 1802, and was elected as a Democratic-Republican to the United States Senate. He was re-elected in 1808 and served from March 4, 1803 until
2 CONC  March 3, 1815. While senator, Smith served as President pro tempore of the Senate during the Ninth and Tenth Congresses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith was elected to the Fourteenth Congress on January 31, 1816 to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of Nicholas R. Moore, and was re-elected to the Fifteenth, Sixteenth, and Seventeenth Congresses. In the House, Smith s
2 CONC erved as chairman of the U.S. House Committee on Expenditures in the Department of the Treasury (Fourteenth Congress), and as a member of the Committee on Ways and Means (Fifteenth through Seventeenth Congresses).
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 17, 1822, Smith resigned as congressman, having been elected as a Democratic Republican (later Crawford Republican and Jacksonian) to the United States Senate to fill the vacancy caused by the death of William Pinkney.
2 CONC  Smith served as President pro tempore of the Senate during the Twentieth and Twenty-first Congresses, and as chairman of the Committee on Finance (Eighteenth and Twentieth through Twenty-second Congresses). He was re-elected in 1
2 CONC 826 and served until March 3, 1833. Two years later, in 1835, Smith became mayor of Baltimore, and served in that position until 1838, when he retired from public life. Smith died in Baltimore in 1839, and is interred in the Old W
2 CONC estminster Burying Ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attitudes to slavery
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1828 Smith served as Vice-President of the Maryland State Colonization Society, of which Charles Carroll of Carrollton, one of the co-signers of the Declaration of Independence, was president.[1] The MSCS was a branch of the Am
2 CONC erican Colonization Society, an organization dedicated to returning black Americans to lead free lives in African states such as Liberia.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Samuel Smith was a United States Senator and Representative from Maryland, a mayor of Baltimore, Maryland, and a general in the Maryland militia. He was the brother of cabinet secretary Robert Smith.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Carlisle, Pennsylvania, Smith moved with his family to Baltimore, Maryland, in 1759. He attended a private academy, and engaged in mercantile pursuits until the American Revolutionary War, at which time he served as captai
2 CONC n, major, and lieutenant colonel in the Continental Army. Prior to the war, as a young captain, he was sent to Annapolis to arrest Governor Eden and seize his papers.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 23 with Philadelphia on the verge of capture, Washington sent Smith, then a Lieutenant Colonel of the 4th Maryland Regiment with a detachment of Continentals into the fort on Mud Island on the Delaware River. Smith's 
2 CONC force numbered 200 soldiers plus Major Robert Ballard of Virginia, Major Simeon Thayer of Rhode Island, and Captain Samuel Treat of the Continental Artillery. However, another account stated that Thayer did not reach Fort Mifflin 
2 CONC until October 19. With the British army closing in on Philadelphia, the small force had to reach Fort Mifflin by a circuitous route. On the last leg of their journey, reinforcements for Mud Island had to be ferried across the Dela
2 CONC ware from Red Bank, New Jersey under the protection of the Pennsylvania Navy river flotilla commanded by John Hazelwood. The fort was eventually overwhelmed by weeks of British bombardment and was abandoned. After the war, Smith e
2 CONC ngaged in the shipping business.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1790 to 1792, Smith was a member of the Maryland House of Delegates. At the time of the threatened war with France in 1794, he was appointed brigadier general of the Maryland militia and commanded Maryland’s quota during the 
2 CONC Whiskey Rebellion. Smith served as a major general of Maryland militia during the War of 1812, and commanded the defenses of Baltimore during the Battle of Baltimore in September 1814. The American victory there can largely be att
2 CONC ributed to Smith's preparation for the British invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith entered into national politics when he was elected to the Third United States Congress, serving from March 4, 1793, until March 4, 1803. As a Congressman, Smith served as chairman of the U.S. House Committee on Commerce and 
2 CONC Manufactures (Fifth through Seventh Congresses). As a principal negotiator between the young Federalist leader and Delaware representative, James Asheton Bayard II, and the presumptive President-Elect Jefferson, Smith secured the 
2 CONC winning ballot in the United States House of Representatives for Jefferson during the United States presidential election, 1800.[6] Smith entered into the Senate election in 1802, and was elected as a Democratic-Republican to the 
2 CONC United States Senate. He was re-elected in 1808 and served from March 4, 1803 until March 4, 1815. While senator, Smith served as President pro tempore of the Senate during the Ninth and Tenth Congresses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith was elected to the Fourteenth Congress on January 31, 1816 to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of Nicholas R. Moore, and was re-elected to the Fifteenth, Sixteenth, and Seventeenth Congresses. In the House, Smith s
2 CONC erved as chairman of the U.S. House Committee on Expenditures in the Department of the Treasury (Fourteenth Congress), and as a member of the Committee on Ways and Means (Fifteenth through Seventeenth Congresses).
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 17, 1822, Smith resigned as congressman, having been elected as a Democratic Republican (later Crawford Republican and Jacksonian) to the United States Senate to fill the vacancy caused by the death of William Pinkney.
2 CONC  In March–April 1824, Samuel Smith was honored with a single vote at the Democratic-Republican Party Caucus to be the party's candidate for U.S. Vice President at the election later that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith served as President pro tempore of the Senate again during the Twentieth and Twenty-first Congresses, and as chairman of the Committee on Finance (Eighteenth and Twentieth through Twenty-second Congresses). He was re-elected
2 CONC  in 1826 and served until March 4, 1833. Two years later, in 1835, Smith became mayor of Baltimore, and served in that position until 1838, when he retired from public life. Smith died in Baltimore in 1839, and is interred in the 
2 CONC Old Westminster Burying Ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1828 Smith served as Vice-President of the Maryland State Colonization Society, of which Charles Carroll of Carrollton, one of the co-signers of the Declaration of Independence, was president. The MSCS was a branch of the Ameri
2 CONC can Colonization Society, an organization dedicated to returning black Americans to lead free lives in African states such as Liberia.
