0 @N6000000002176663537@ NOTE He divided his empire into the Dual Monarchy, in which Austria and Hungary
1 CONT coexisted as equal partners. In 1879 he formed an alliance with Prussian-led
1 CONT Germany, and in 1914 his ultimatum to Serbia led Austria and Germany into
1 CONT World War I. Austria passed through a long Metamorphosis with its many ups
1 CONT and downs in the 68 years that he occupied the throne. His many mistakes were
1 CONT balanced by splendid achievements which bore the stamp of the Emperor's own
1 CONT silent devotion to duty, wearing a soldier's uniform all through his life.
0 @N6000000002188402865@ NOTE Franz Josef was crowned Emperor of Austria in 1848 at age 18. By the summer of 1914 he would be in the 66th year of his reign. He was also crowned King of Hungary in 1867 in an attempt to calm the situation with the problematic Ma
1 CONC gyars (Hungarians). This worked and the Dual Monarchy would last until his death in 1916.  The aging monarch had seen the Habsburg Empire lose its holdings in both Italy and Germany until it had become mainly an eastern European p
1 CONC ower. Franz Josef was sensitive to these losses and was determined not to allow further decay of the empire by losing Austro-Hungarian holdings in the Balkans to Serbia. It has been said that he was "the last monarch of the old sc
1 CONC hool".  Despite these political beliefs he was immensely popular among all the various national groups that comprised his kingdom. This can be attributed to the above average standard of living that his subjects enjoyed. By 1914 h
1 CONC e rarely left his palaces but it was not out of fear of assassination.  Emperor and King, still his life was not without its trials and sorrows. Early on as Emperor he lost major wars to France (1848) and Prussia (1866). His broth
1 CONC er, Maximilian, was executed in Mexico. His son, Crown Prince Rudolph, committed suicide in 1889 followed by his wife's assassination by an anarchist in Geneva (1897). He had numerous difficulties with his nephew and heir, Archduk
1 CONC e Franz Ferdinand, who would be assassinated by Serbian nationalists on 28-Jun-1914 at a place called Sarajevo.
