0 @N6000000005598973274@ NOTE Caroline Matilda of Wales From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caroline Matilda of Wales (July 11, 1751 - May 10, 1775), was a princess of the United Kingdom and Ireland, sister of King George III of the United Kingdom and Queen of Denmark from 1767 to 1772.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caroline Matilda was the youngest child of Frederick, Prince of Wales and Augusta of Saxe-Gotha, hence her title. Her father died suddenly about three months before her birth. She was born at Leicester House in London, and was giv
1 CONC en the title Princess Caroline Matilda of Wales as daughter of the Prince of Wales -- though, by the time of her birth, the title of Prince of Wales had passed to her brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the age of fifteen, Caroline Mathilde, as she was known in Denmark, was married to her cousin, King Christian VII of Denmark and Norway, and left her family behind in Britain to travel to Denmark. Her brother was anxious about 
1 CONC her marriage, even though he was unaware of the full facts about the bridegroom, who was mentally ill. In 1768, she became mother of the Crown Prince, later to become King Frederick VI of Denmark and Norway. Another child, Princes
1 CONC s Louise Augusta, was born in 1771.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due mainly to King Christian's illness, Caroline Mathilde was neglected in her marriage, which caused her to enter in a love affair with the royal physician and minister Johann Friedrich Struensee; he was almost certainly the fath
1 CONC er of Princess Louise Augusta. When Struensee's attempts to undermine the king failed and he was executed in 1772, Caroline Mathilde was divorced and deported to neighbouring Celle in Germany. She did not give up hope of returning
1 CONC  to Denmark and seeing her ex-husband deposed, but her indiscreet behaviour dismayed her brother, and he was reluctant to have her back in England, even if she had been willing to return. She died suddenly from throat cancer, at C
1 CONC elle, in 1775.
0 @N6000000006127657195@ NOTE Caroline Mathilde föddes i juli 1751 på Leicester House i London som dotter till prinsen av Wales Frederick Louis av Hannover (1707-1751) och Augusta av Saxe-Gotha (1719-1772). Caroline var syster till Storbritanniens kung Georg I
1 CONC II. Carolines fardog fyra månader innan hon föddes.När hon var femton år gammal, den 8 november 1766, gifte hon sig med sin kusin, Christian VII av Oldenburg, som då var sjutton år, och vid 16½ års ålder, den 28 januari 1768 födde
1 CONC  hon sonen Fredrik (VI).Hennes man förde ett utsvävande liv, och blev förstörd både till kropp och själ, samt att han var helt ointresserad av henne. Han led troligtvis också av schizofreni, stundvis hade han anfall av ren galensk
1 CONC ap.Vid hemkomsten från en utrikes resa (januari 1769) hade hennes man med sig Johann Friedrich Struensee, som var hans livläkare. Denna begåvade man fick snart stort inflytande över drottningen, och på våren 1770 gick förtrolighet
1 CONC en över till ettkärleksförhållande, vilket Caroline Mathilde inte gjorde mycket för att dölja. Detta väckte allmän förargelse hos folket.År 1771 födde hon dottern Lovisa Augusta, som troligtvis var Struensees dotter och inte henne
1 CONC s man Kristians. Inom kort blev Struensee den mäktigaste mannen i Danmark, men palatsrevolutionen den 17 januari 1772 störtade både honom och CarolineMathilde från makten.Hon fördes som fånge till Kronborgs slott tillsammans med s
1 CONC in lilla dotter Lovisa Augusta (senare gift med hertig Fredrik Christian av Augustenborg), och hon bekände där det brottsliga förhållande, som hon haft till Struensee. Genom en kommittésdom den 6 april 1772 upplöstes hennes äktens
1 CONC kap med Christian VII.Den 28 april 1772 blev hennes älskare Struensee halshuggen, och den 30 maj fördes hon själv på ett engelskt krigsfartyg till Hannover. Hon fick aldrig se sina två barn igen. Hon tillbringade sin sista tid på 
1 CONC slottet i Celle, som ligger i den tyskadelstaten Niedersachsen, där hon visade stor välgörenhet mot stadens sjuka och fattiga. Hon dog den 10 maj 1775, endast 23 år och 9 månader gammal. Gravplats: Tyskland, Wittenberg - Stadtkirc
1 CONC he St. Marien  
0 @N6000000007329600941@ NOTE [Kopi av ROYALS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Born Posthumously.
0 @N6000000002188464848@ NOTE As a young officer, the Duke fought successfully on the side of Prince Eugène of Savoy (1663-1736) against the Turks.  Karl I had to take up the rule in the principality of Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel unexpectedly early.  His father
1 CONC , Duke Ferdinand Albrecht II. (1680-1735), the first ruling duke in Wolfenbüttel from the line of Bevern, died after just a six-month reign.  As a result of the friendship of his father with the soldier King Friedrich Wilhelm I (1
1 CONC 688-1740) of Prussia, Karl I had already married the Prussian princess Philippine Charlotte in 1733.  She was a sister of Friedrich II the Great (1712-1786) of Prussia.  As a result of the simultaneous marriage of Karl's sister, P
1 CONC rincess Elisabeth Christine of Braunschweig, to the future King Friedrich II, the friendly alliance of the two dynasties was underlined.  In complete contrast to Friedrich II. of Prussia, the marriage of Karl I with Philippine Cha
1 CONC rlotte was generally happy.  From his marriage there came thirteen children.  Under the leadership of the theologian Jerusalem, Duke Karl founded the Collegium Carolinum in Brunswick in 1745.  This type of school, which became the
1 CONC  standard in  Germany, became a link between the old Latin school and the university.  The students received a modern education in the sense of the enlightenment.  Even at the beginning of his reign the absolute ruling Duke Karl I
1 CONC  had to accommodate government dealings and his luxurious royal household to the catastrophic financial situation of the principality.  Attempts to reform the economy foundered on a poorly trained civil service apparatus, or they 
1 CONC were rejected by the estates of the realm.  Thus the duke came to favor the establishment of a new management in the principality.  Well-intentioned measures for advancing the court were not, however, able to cover over the failur
1 CONC es. For financial reasons the royal household had to be moved from Wolfenbüttel to Braunschweig.  Above all, the Seven Years' War (1756-1763) had a negative impact on the principality of Braunschweig. In 1773 hereditary prince Kar
1 CONC l Wilhelm Ferdinand (1735-1806) took over the business of ruling and deprived his father of power as a result of the financial misery in the land.
0 @N6000000001562994301@ NOTE George Augustus Frederick, born 12 August 1762, was created Prince of Wales in 1762.  In 1785 he married Maria Anne Fitzherbert, a Roman Catholic. The marriage was illegal, however; and in 1795, to secure parliamentary settlement 
1 CONC of his enormous debts, he made a political marriage with Caroline of Brunswick.  He married Princess Caroline of Brunswick (1768-1821) in 1795.  He ascended to the thrones of Great Britain and Hanover, as King George IV, on 29 Jan
1 CONC uary, 1820, and was crowned on 19 July 1821. He died 26 June 1830.
1 CONT
1 CONT The couple had one child: Charlotte, born 7 January, 1796. Princess Charlotte married Prince Leopold of Saxe-Coburg on 2 May, 1816. She died in childbirth of a stillborn son on 6 November 1817.
1 CONT
1 CONT In constant and open opposition to his father, George associated closely with the Whigs.  As a result, when George III had his first serious fit of insanity in 1788–89, the Tory William Pitt proposed that the regency vested in the
1 CONC  prince be closely restricted (to prevent George bringing his Whig friends to power), while Fox, usually the opponent of royal prerogative, wanted the prince to have unlimited powers as regent.  In 1811, after the king had become 
1 CONC permanently incapacitated, George became regent on terms very similar to those proposed by Pitt in 1788.  However, when the limitations on his power to make appointments and spend crown revenues were removed in 1812, the prince re
1 CONC gent retained most of his father's ministers, breaking his connection with the Whigs. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The Tories, under the leadership of the 2nd Earl of Liverpool for most of the period, remained entrenched in power throughout the regency and George's subsequent reign.  As regent and as king, George was hated for his extravagance
1 CONC  and dissolute habits, and he aroused particular hostility by an unsuccessful attempt, immediately after his accession (1820) to the throne, to divorce his long-estranged wife, Caroline.  During his reign the monarchy lost a signi
1 CONC ficant amount of power.  George was succeeded by his brother William IV	
1 CONT
1 CONT George IV, eldest son of George III and Charlotte, was born August 12, 1762. He secretly married his first wife, the Catholic widow Maria Fitzherbert, in 1785 without his father's permission. The marriage was declared illegal at h
1 CONC is father's behest; had the marriage been allowed to continue, George would have been ineligible to reign with a Catholic wife. In 1795, he married again, this time to his cousin Caroline of Brunswick, who bore him one daughter, C
1 CONC harlotte. He died on June 26, 1830 after a series of strokes brought on a hemorrhage in his stomach. 
1 CONT George IV was the antithesis of his father: conservative in his infrequent political involvement and licentious in affairs of the heart. Although he was scandalous with his mistresses and extravagant in his spending, he was a patr
1 CONC on of the arts who left many wonderful artifacts for posterity. He had his father's immense book collection donated as the foundation of the British Museum Library and his penchant for building projects inspired the "Regency" styl
1 CONC e of architecture. His extravagances, however, came at a time of social distress and general misery following the Napoleonic Wars and the tremendous changes brought forth by the industrial revolution. 
1 CONT George's amorous nature was highly controversial. As Prince Regent, he had many mistresses until he secretly married Maria. After her dismissal from court, George again turned to mistresses until he submitted to his father's wishe
1 CONC s by marrying Caroline. The couple detested each other and their marriagewas barely intact when their daughter was born in 1796. Caroline took the child and moved to Italy, returning to England when George succeeded his father, an
1 CONC d then only to claim the rights of queen. George managed to have her barred from his coronation, denying her queenship. 
1 CONT George was an enigma: bright, witty and able on the one hand, indolent, spoiled, and lazy on the other. The Duke of Wellington described him as such: "He was the most extraordinary compound of talent, wit, buffoonery, obstinacy, a
1 CONC nd good feelings, in short, a medley of the most opposite qualities, with a great preponderance of good - that I ever saw in any character in my life."
0 @N6000000001579393904@ NOTE George Augustus Frederick, born 12 August 1762, was created Prince of Wales in 1762.  In 1785 he married Maria Anne Fitzherbert, a Roman Catholic. The marriage was illegal, however; and in 1795, to secure parliamentary settlement 
1 CONC of his enormous debts, he made a political marriage with Caroline of Brunswick.  He married Princess Caroline of Brunswick (1768-1821) in 1795.  He ascended to the thrones of Great Britain and Hanover, as King George IV, on 29 Jan
1 CONC uary, 1820, and was crowned on 19 July 1821. He died 26 June 1830.
1 CONT
1 CONT The couple had one child: Charlotte, born 7 January, 1796. Princess Charlotte married Prince Leopold of Saxe-Coburg on 2 May, 1816. She died in childbirth of a stillborn son on 6 November 1817.
1 CONT
1 CONT In constant and open opposition to his father, George associated closely with the Whigs.  As a result, when George III had his first serious fit of insanity in 1788–89, the Tory William Pitt proposed that the regency vested in the
1 CONC  prince be closely restricted (to prevent George bringing his Whig friends to power), while Fox, usually the opponent of royal prerogative, wanted the prince to have unlimited powers as regent.  In 1811, after the king had become 
1 CONC permanently incapacitated, George became regent on terms very similar to those proposed by Pitt in 1788.  However, when the limitations on his power to make appointments and spend crown revenues were removed in 1812, the prince re
1 CONC gent retained most of his father's ministers, breaking his connection with the Whigs.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Tories, under the leadership of the 2nd Earl of Liverpool for most of the period, remained entrenched in power throughout the regency and George's subsequent reign.  As regent and as king, George was hated for his extravagance
1 CONC  and dissolute habits, and he aroused particular hostility by an unsuccessful attempt, immediately after his accession (1820) to the throne, to divorce his long-estranged wife, Caroline.  During his reign the monarchy lost a signi
1 CONC ficant amount of power.  George was succeeded by his brother William IV	
1 CONT
1 CONT George IV, eldest son of George III and Charlotte, was born August 12, 1762. He secretly married his first wife, the Catholic widow Maria Fitzherbert, in 1785 without his father's permission. The marriage was declared illegal at h
1 CONC is father's behest; had the marriage been allowed to continue, George would have been ineligible to reign with a Catholic wife. In 1795, he married again, this time to his cousin Caroline of Brunswick, who bore him one daughter, C
1 CONC harlotte. He died on June 26, 1830 after a series of strokes brought on a hemorrhage in his stomach.
1 CONT George IV was the antithesis of his father: conservative in his infrequent political involvement and licentious in affairs of the heart. Although he was scandalous with his mistresses and extravagant in his spending, he was a patr
1 CONC on of the arts who left many wonderful artifacts for posterity. He had his father's immense book collection donated as the foundation of the British Museum Library and his penchant for building projects inspired the "Regency" styl
1 CONC e of architecture. His extravagances, however, came at a time of social distress and general misery following the Napoleonic Wars and the tremendous changes brought forth by the industrial revolution.
1 CONT George's amorous nature was highly controversial. As Prince Regent, he had many mistresses until he secretly married Maria. After her dismissal from court, George again turned to mistresses until he submitted to his father's wishe
1 CONC s by marrying Caroline. The couple detested each other and their marriagewas barely intact when their daughter was born in 1796. Caroline took the child and moved to Italy, returning to England when George succeeded his father, an
1 CONC d then only to claim the rights of queen. George managed to have her barred from his coronation, denying her queenship.
1 CONT George was an enigma: bright, witty and able on the one hand, indolent, spoiled, and lazy on the other. The Duke of Wellington described him as such: "He was the most extraordinary compound of talent, wit, buffoonery, obstinacy, a
1 CONC nd good feelings, in short, a medley of the most opposite qualities, with a great preponderance of good - that I ever saw in any character in my life."
0 @N6000000002322337372@ NOTE He ruled from 1820-1830, and was King of Hannover from 1820-1830. his
1 CONT marriage to Maria Anne was short-lived, for she was a Catholic, and He
1 CONT allowed Parliament to declare the marriage illegal. In 1795, he married his
1 CONT cousin, Caroline of Brunswick. This marriage did not go well. He deserted
1 CONT Caroline from 1813 on, and she lived much of her time in Italy. George was
1 CONT ultimately succeeded by his brother, William IV; the third son of George III.
0 @N6000000002188395365@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      George IV debased the image of British monarchy to a level never reached before, using his position merely as the instrument of living in self-indulgence.  If he had had that power of political murder which he envied in Henry
1 CONC  VIII he might well have used it, but his only positive political action was reaction, particularly the blocking of political concessions to Roman Catholics.  His life provided the strongest pragmatical argument against the princi
1 CONC ple of hereditary sovereignty, and British culture could only be contaminated by his label of First Gentleman in Europe.
